## Directory structure

```text
./.config
└── nvim
    ├── init.lua
    └── lua
        ├── config
        │   ├── keymaps.lua
        │   └── options.lua
        └── plugins
            ├── fugitive.lua
            ├── lspconfig.lua
            ├── lualine.lua
            ├── neo-tree.lua
            ├── oil.lua
            ├── telescope.lua
            └── treesitter.lua
```

| File / directory | Purpose |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `init.lua` | Main entry point; loads configuration modules and plugins. |
| `lua/config/options.lua` | Editor settings: indentation, line numbers, search, splits and display. |
| `lua/config/keymaps.lua` | Global keyboard shortcuts and leader key configuration. |
| `lua/plugins/` | Individual plugin installation and configuration modules. |

Each plugin is configured independently, making it easier to maintain, customize or disable individual features.

## Requirements

| Dependency | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| [Neovim](https://neovim.io/) 0.12+ | Editor, Lua runtime and native plugin management. |
| `git` | Plugin installation and version control. |
| `ripgrep` (`rg`) | Text search through Telescope. |
| `fd` or `fdfind` | Fast file discovery with Telescope (optional). |
| C compiler, `curl`, `tar` | Tree-sitter parser installation. |
| `tree-sitter` CLI | Parser tooling; see the selected Tree-sitter branch's requirements. |
| Language servers | LSP features for the languages being edited. |
| [Nerd Font](https://www.nerdfonts.com/) | Recommended for displaying file and status icons in the terminal. |

### Debian / Ubuntu

```sh
sudo apt update
sudo apt install git ripgrep fd-find build-essential curl tar
```

### Alpine Linux

```sh
sudo apk add git ripgrep fd build-base curl tar
```

Install Neovim 0.12 or later and a compatible Tree-sitter CLI separately if the distribution packages are too old. The Tree-sitter `main` branch currently requires CLI 0.26.1 or later. Install the font on the machine running the terminal, not necessarily on the remote Linux host.

Telescope and Neo-tree use `plenary.nvim`. Neo-tree also uses `nui.nvim`; icon providers such as `nvim-web-devicons` are optional depending on the configuration. These plugin dependencies must be declared in the Lua configuration as needed.

## Installation

From the root of this dotfiles repository, run the following commands. Back up an existing Neovim configuration before copying the new one.

```sh
mkdir -p "$HOME/.config"

if [ -e "$HOME/.config/nvim" ] || [ -L "$HOME/.config/nvim" ] 
then
    mv "$HOME/.config/nvim" "$HOME/.config/nvim.backup-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"
fi

cp -R .config/nvim "$HOME/.config/"

nvim
```

On the first launch, `vim.pack.add()` calls in the loaded Lua modules install missing plugins. Internet access may be required. Install the relevant language servers and Tree-sitter parsers before using their features.

Check the installation from inside Neovim:

```vim
:checkhealth
```

> The paths above assume the standard Linux XDG configuration directory (`~/.config`).

## Plugin overview

| Plugin | Configuration | Main purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [vim-fugitive](https://github.com/tpope/vim-fugitive) | `fugitive.lua` | Git integration |
| [nvim-lspconfig](https://github.com/neovim/nvim-lspconfig) | `lspconfig.lua` | Language server configurations |
| [lualine.nvim](https://github.com/nvim-lualine/lualine.nvim) | `lualine.lua` | Informative status line |
| [neo-tree.nvim](https://github.com/nvim-neo-tree/neo-tree.nvim) | `neo-tree.lua` | Tree-based project explorer |
| [oil.nvim](https://github.com/stevearc/oil.nvim) | `oil.lua` | Buffer-based file management |
| [telescope.nvim](https://github.com/nvim-telescope/telescope.nvim) | `telescope.lua` | Interactive fuzzy search |
| [nvim-treesitter](https://github.com/nvim-treesitter/nvim-treesitter) | `treesitter.lua` | Syntax-aware editing |

## Plugin usage

The following reference describes upstream commands and commonly available default mappings. Buffer-local shortcuts apply only inside the relevant plugin window. Local Lua settings may override these defaults.

**Notation:** `<CR>` = Enter, `<Esc>` = Escape, `<C-x>` = Ctrl+x, `<Tab>` = Tab, `<leader>` = configured leader key. Unless otherwise noted, mappings are used in Normal mode.

### 1. Fugitive — Git integration

Work with Git repositories without leaving Neovim: inspect changes, stage files, commit, review history and compare revisions.

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `:Git` | Open the repository status window. |
| `:Git diff` | View unstaged changes. |
| `:Git log` | Browse commit history. |
| `:Git blame` | Show commit attribution for the current file. |
| `:Gdiffsplit` | Compare the current file in a split. |
| `:Git commit` | Commit staged changes. |
| `:Git pull` / `:Git push` | Synchronize with a remote repository. |

**Inside the Git status window:**

| Key | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `<CR>` | Open the selected item. |
| `s` / `u` | Stage / unstage the selected file or hunk. |
| `=` | Toggle the inline diff. |
| `cc` | Start a commit. |
| `g?` | Display mapping help. |

Typical workflow: `:Git` → select changed files → `s` → `cc` → write the commit message → `:wq` → `:Git push` when ready.

Help: `:help fugitive`

### 2. LSPConfig — Language Server Protocol

Provides ready-made language server configurations for Neovim's native LSP client. Features can include diagnostics, symbol navigation, documentation, refactoring, completion and formatting, depending on the language server.

Language servers are separate executables and are not installed automatically by `nvim-lspconfig`.

| Language | Example server / configuration name |
| --- | --- |
| Python | Pyright (`pyright`), Ruff (`ruff`) |
| Lua | Lua Language Server (`lua_ls`) |
| Bash | Bash Language Server (`bashls`) |
| JSON | VS Code JSON Language Server (`jsonls`) |
| YAML | YAML Language Server (`yamlls`) |

Install the servers required by `lspconfig.lua`, ensure their executables are on `PATH`, and enable the relevant configuration using the modern Neovim API:

```lua
-- Example: requires Pyright to be installed separately.
vim.lsp.enable('pyright')
```

**Common native LSP mappings:**

| Key | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `K` | Display documentation for the symbol under the cursor. |
| `grr` | Find references. |
| `gri` | Find implementations. |
| `grn` | Rename a symbol. |
| `gra` | Show code actions (Normal or Visual mode). |
| `grt` | Go to type definition. |
| `[d` / `]d` | Previous / next diagnostic. |
| `<C-x><C-o>` | Request omni-completion in Insert mode, when configured. |

Other useful actions, available when supported by the attached server:

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `:lua vim.lsp.buf.definition()` | Go to the definition. |
| `:lua vim.lsp.buf.format()` | Format the buffer. |
| `:lua vim.diagnostic.open_float()` | Display diagnostic details. |
| `:checkhealth vim.lsp` | Inspect LSP configuration and clients. |

Help: `:help lsp`, `:help lsp-defaults`, `:help lspconfig-all`

### 3. Lualine — Status line

Displays contextual information at the bottom of the editor, such as the current mode, Git branch, file name, diagnostic counts, file type and cursor position.

A typical status line contains:

```text
NORMAL | main | init.lua [+] | lua | utf-8 | 42:8
```

Lualine is primarily informational and has no essential interactive shortcuts. Themes, sections, separators and displayed components are configured in `lualine.lua`.

Help: `:help lualine.txt`

### 4. Neo-tree — Project explorer

Provides a sidebar for browsing directories, open buffers and Git changes. Useful for understanding the structure of a project and performing file operations.

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `:Neotree toggle` | Show or hide the explorer. |
| `:Neotree reveal` | Locate the current file in the tree. |
| `:Neotree buffers` | Display open buffers. |
| `:Neotree git_status` | Display Git changes. |

**Inside the explorer:**

| Key | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `j` / `k` | Move down / up. |
| `<CR>` | Open the selected file or directory. |
| `a` | Create an item. |
| `r` | Rename an item. |
| `d` | Delete an item. |
| `y` / `x` / `p` | Copy / cut / paste an item. |
| `s` / `S` | Open in a vertical / horizontal split. |
| `?` | Show mappings. |

Help: `:help neo-tree`

### 5. Oil — Filesystem editing

Displays a directory as an editable Neovim buffer. Create, rename, move, copy and delete filesystem entries using familiar editing operations.

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `:Oil` | Open the current file's directory. |
| `:Oil .` | Open the working directory. |
| `:Oil --float` | Open the directory in a floating window. |

**Inside an Oil buffer:**

| Key / command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `<CR>` | Open a file or enter a directory. |
| `-` | Navigate to the parent directory. |
| `g.` | Toggle hidden files. |
| `g?` | Show available mappings. |
| `dd` | Remove an entry from the buffer. |
| `:w` | Apply pending filesystem changes. |

To rename a file, edit its name directly and save the buffer. To create a file, insert a new line containing its name; append `/` for a directory. Review the proposed operations before confirming, especially deletions and moves.

Help: `:help oil`, `:help oil-actions`

### 6. Telescope — Fuzzy finder

Search for files, text, buffers, help topics and Git information using interactive pickers with previews.

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `:Telescope find_files` | Find files by name. |
| `:Telescope live_grep` | Search text across the working directory. |
| `:Telescope grep_string` | Search for the word under the cursor. |
| `:Telescope buffers` | Switch between open buffers. |
| `:Telescope oldfiles` | Browse recently opened files. |
| `:Telescope git_files` | Find Git-tracked files. |
| `:Telescope help_tags` | Search Neovim documentation. |
| `:Telescope keymaps` | Search configured key mappings. |
| `:Telescope diagnostics` | Browse diagnostics. |

**Inside a picker:**

| Key | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `<C-n>` / `<C-p>` | Next / previous result. |
| `<CR>` | Open the selected result. |
| `<C-x>` / `<C-v>` | Open in a horizontal / vertical split. |
| `<C-t>` | Open in a new tab. |
| `<C-u>` / `<C-d>` | Scroll the preview up / down. |
| `<Tab>` | Toggle selection and move to the next result. |
| `<C-q>` | Send matching results to the quickfix list. |
| `<C-c>` | Close the picker in Insert mode. |
| `<Esc>` | Close the picker in Normal mode. |
| `?` | Show picker mappings in Normal mode. |

Run Neovim from the project root for convenient project-wide searching. `live_grep` requires `ripgrep`.

Help: `:help telescope.nvim`, `:help telescope.builtin`

### 7. Tree-sitter — Syntax-aware editing

Installs language parsers and queries used by Neovim to understand code structure. This supports syntax highlighting, language injections, folding and, where configured, experimental indentation.

Tree-sitter performs syntactic analysis locally, whereas LSP provides language-server-powered semantic features such as references and diagnostics. They complement each other.

| Command | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `:TSInstall python` | Install the Python parser and queries. |
| `:TSInstall bash lua json yaml` | Install several parsers. |
| `:TSUpdate` | Update installed parsers. |
| `:TSUninstall python` | Remove the Python parser. |
| `:Inspect` | Inspect highlighting and semantic information under the cursor. |
| `:InspectTree` | Display the syntax tree of the current buffer. |

For the rewritten `main` branch, installing a parser alone does not enable highlighting. The configuration should activate it for the relevant file types using `vim.treesitter.start()`. Folding and indentation require additional configuration.

There are no universal Tree-sitter-specific editing shortcuts in this setup. If Tree-sitter folding is configured, the standard Neovim fold mappings `za`, `zo` and `zc` toggle, open and close folds respectively.

Help: `:help treesitter`, `:help nvim-treesitter-commands`

## Suggested shortcuts

The following global Normal-mode mappings are a proposed convention, **not verified mappings from the repository**. Add or adapt them in `keymaps.lua` or the relevant plugin modules. Set the leader key before defining mappings; for example, `vim.g.mapleader = ' '` uses Space.

| Shortcut | Action / command |
| --- | --- |
| `<leader>ff` | Find files — `:Telescope find_files` |
| `<leader>fg` | Search text — `:Telescope live_grep` |
| `<leader>fb` | Find buffers — `:Telescope buffers` |
| `<leader>fh` | Find help — `:Telescope help_tags` |
| `<leader>fr` | Recent files — `:Telescope oldfiles` |
| `<leader>e`  | Toggle Neo-tree — `:Neotree toggle` |
| `<leader>o`  | Open Oil — `:Oil` |
| `<leader>gs` | Git status — `:Git` |
| `<leader>gb` | Git blame — `:Git blame` |
| `<leader>gd` | Git diff split — `:Gdiffsplit` |
| `gd`         | Go to definition — `vim.lsp.buf.definition()` |
| `<leader>lf` | Format — `vim.lsp.buf.format()` |
| `<leader>ld` | Diagnostic details — `vim.diagnostic.open_float()` |

Example mapping definitions:

```lua
vim.g.mapleader = ' '

vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>ff', '<cmd>Telescope find_files<cr>',
    { desc = 'Find files' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>fg', '<cmd>Telescope live_grep<cr>',
    { desc = 'Search project text' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>e', '<cmd>Neotree toggle<cr>',
    { desc = 'Toggle file explorer' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>o', '<cmd>Oil<cr>',
    { desc = 'Open directory' })
vim.keymap.set('n', '<leader>gs', '<cmd>Git<cr>',
    { desc = 'Git status' })
```

To inspect active mappings, use `:map`, `:nmap`, `:verbose nmap <leader>ff` or `:Telescope keymaps`.

## Essential Neovim commands

| Command / key | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `:w` / `:q` / `:wq` | Save / quit / save and quit. |
| `:e <file>` | Open a file. |
| `:ls` | List buffers. |
| `:bn` / `:bp` | Next / previous buffer. |
| `:bd` | Delete the current buffer from the buffer list. |
| `:split` / `:vsplit` | Horizontal / vertical split. |
| `<C-w>h/j/k/l` | Move between windows. |
| `:terminal` | Open an integrated terminal. |
| `<C-\\><C-n>` | Leave Terminal mode for Normal mode. |
| `:copen` | Open the quickfix list. |
| `:cnext` / `:cprev` | Next / previous quickfix entry. |

## Maintenance and troubleshooting

### Update plugins

```vim
:lua vim.pack.update()
```

Review the proposed changes and confirm the update. After updating Tree-sitter, synchronize the parsers:

```vim
:TSUpdate
```

Restart Neovim and check for errors. For reproducible installations, consider committing the generated `nvim-pack-lock.json` lockfile alongside the configuration when using `vim.pack`.

### Health checks

| Command | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `:checkhealth` | Overall installation health. |
| `:checkhealth vim.lsp` | Language server availability and status. |
| `:checkhealth nvim-treesitter` | Parser and dependency checks. |
| `:checkhealth telescope` | Telescope dependencies. |
| `:messages` | Startup messages, warnings and errors. |
| `:verbose nmap <leader>ff` | Identify the source of a mapping. |
| `:lua print(vim.fn.stdpath('config'))` | Locate the active configuration. |

If icons render incorrectly, select a Nerd Font in the terminal settings. If LSP does not attach, check server installation, file type, project root markers and the enabled configuration. If text search fails, check that `rg` is on `PATH`.

### Documentation

- [Neovim user manual](https://neovim.io/doc/user/)
- [Native package management (`vim.pack`)](https://neovim.io/doc/user/pack/)
